#!/usr/bin/env bash
#MISE description="The pure decision behind plan-hold-guard: is a background hold live, so a turn that hands control to a human will not end idle?"
#
# CLOUD-451. Handing control to a human — `ExitPlanMode`, `AskUserQuestion` — is
# the one turn end that is CORRECTLY idle: nothing is running because nothing
# should be, the session is waiting on a person. The container reclaims idle
# sessions anyway, and the cost lands on the human rather than the agent: text
# already typed into the approval box is discarded, or the plan closes and
# reopens empty. The longer someone reads before approving, the likelier their
# input is destroyed, which makes careful review the *punished* behaviour.
#
# `run-shape-guard` already names the failure ("an idle turn gets the VM
# reclaimed") and AGENTS.md carries it as a rule. Both are about the agent's own
# idleness and neither reaches this boundary, so the rule shipped without its
# mechanism (non-negotiable 2). `plan-hold` is the mechanism; this is its
# predicate, split out for the same reason `memory-guard-check` is split from
# `memory-guard`: the decision is then directly callable and directly testable,
# and the sentinel location is spelled exactly once, here.
#
# **A hold is live when a sentinel names a pid that still answers.** Two parts,
# never one. A bare "the file exists" would let a killed hold — a reclaimed
# container, a `land` that took the process down — read as protection that is not
# there, which is worse than no guard at all because it is silent. This is the
# same corpse-versus-free-lock distinction `alive` draws over `batten-tasks` and
# `target-ensure` draws over its lock, and the asymmetry is chosen the same way:
# here a dead hold read as LIVE is the expensive direction, because it licenses
# exactly the idle handoff this exists to prevent. So an unevaluable probe reads
# as dead, and the cost of being wrong is one redundant `mise run plan-hold`.
#
# **Reporting a corpse also reaps it** (again `alive`'s shape), so the hold
# directory is self-healing rather than an accumulating pile of headstones that
# would each need a `kill -0` on every future call.
#
# One sentinel per PID, not one per session. `CLAUDE_SESSION_ID` is not reliably
# exported into a task's environment, and a single per-repo path would have two
# concurrent sessions overwriting each other's record. A file named by pid needs
# no identity plumbing at all, and "is anything holding this clone open" is the
# question the guard actually asks.
#
# Usage: plan-hold-check live      — exit 0 live / 1 not live / 2 cannot look
#        plan-hold-check dir       — print the hold directory (for plan-hold and
#                                    plan-hold-release, so the path has one home)
#
# Exit codes are §7's: 0 the predicate holds, 1 it does not, 2 the question could
# not be asked. There is deliberately no exit code for "reaped something" — that
# is bookkeeping, not a verdict.
set -uo pipefail

# The one authoritative spelling of the hold location. Under `$GIT_DIR` rather
# than `$TMPDIR` because that is where this repo already keeps per-clone
# machinery (`batten-receipts`, `batten-tasks`), and because `claim-guard`
# already treats the git dir as machinery rather than work — so a hold can be
# armed inside plan mode without a write to any tracked path.
# ABSOLUTE, deliberately: `--git-dir` answers `.git` from the repo root, and this
# path is consumed by hooks whose working directory is the harness's rather than
# the one the hold was armed from. A relative answer would have two callers
# resolving the same hold to different directories, which is a false not-live —
# the reading that licenses the idle handoff.
hold_dir() {
	local git_dir
	git_dir=$(git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir 2>/dev/null) || return 1
	[ -n "$git_dir" ] || return 1
	printf '%s\n' "$git_dir/batten-holds"
}

mode="${1:-live}"

case "$mode" in
dir)
	dir=$(hold_dir) || {
		echo "::error:: plan-hold-check: not a git repository — no hold directory" >&2
		exit 2
	}
	printf '%s\n' "$dir"
	exit 0
	;;
live) ;;
*)
	echo "::error:: plan-hold-check: unknown mode \"$mode\" (want: live | dir)" >&2
	exit 2
	;;
esac

dir=$(hold_dir) || {
	echo "::error:: plan-hold-check: not a git repository — cannot say whether a hold is live" >&2
	exit 2
}

# The refusal text, written once. Both the not-live exits below reach it, so the
# fix a caller is told about cannot drift between them — a deny that does not
# carry its fix is the shape `refusal.rs` exists to make impossible, and this is
# the shell side keeping the same bargain.
#
# Pointer-only (non-negotiable 4): a command and a path. Never a plan, never a
# question, never a line of whatever the session was about to ask.
not_live() {
	cat <<'FIX'
No background hold is live, so ending this turn would leave the container idle
while a human reads — and an idle container is reclaimed, destroying whatever
they had already typed.

Arm one first, in its own tool call, with run_in_background:

  mise run plan-hold

It prints nothing until it is released, costs no tokens while it waits, and
exits within one poll of the human's reply — that exit is the wake-up. Bypass
with BATTEN_PLAN_HOLD_BYPASS=1 when ending the turn idle is genuinely intended.
FIX
	exit 1
}

# Never created, so nothing has ever held this clone open. That is a real answer
# — not live — and NOT the same as being unable to look, which is exit 2 above.
[ -d "$dir" ] || not_live

# Present but unreadable is "could not look". Distinguished from absence for the
# same reason `alive` distinguishes them: collapsing the two would report a
# broken registry as a confident verdict.
[ -r "$dir" ] && [ -x "$dir" ] || {
	echo "::error:: plan-hold-check: hold directory at $dir is unreadable — cannot say whether a hold is live" >&2
	exit 2
}

live=0
# Lexical glob order, so repeated calls over the same state are byte-stable
# (non-negotiable 5) rather than dependent on readdir order.
for file in "$dir"/*; do
	[ -f "$file" ] || continue
	pid=$(head -n 1 -- "$file" 2>/dev/null) || continue
	# A half-written record is not a hold. Inventing one from a blank or a
	# non-numeric line would be a claim, so it is reaped like a corpse.
	case "$pid" in
	'' | *[!0-9]*)
		rm -f -- "$file" 2>/dev/null || true
		continue
		;;
	esac
	if kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null; then
		live=$((live + 1))
	else
		rm -f -- "$file" 2>/dev/null || true
	fi
done

[ "$live" -gt 0 ] || not_live

# Pointer-only on the allow path too: a count, never a pid list and never what
# the hold is holding for.
echo "plan-hold-check: $live hold(s) live"
